One common vulnerability in Java web applications is **Insecure Deserialization**, often caused by using the `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` method on untrusted data. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code if malicious objects are sent to the application.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class VulnerableServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(8080);
        System.out.println("Server listening on 8080...");

        while (true) {
            try (Socket socket = server.accept();
                 ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {

                // This line is vulnerable to Deserialization attacks, 
                // as it will instantiate any class present on the classpath 
                // provided in the byte stream.
                Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
                System.out.println("Received: " + obj.toString());

            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
